-### [Extended Marching Cubes with Error Information](11167-extended-marching-cubes-with-error-information.md)
-This method extends MC to incorporate local error information (e.g., from wavelet decomposition) into the iso-surface representation, interpolating error metrics alongside geometric coordinates. It differs from siblings by explicitly carrying and interpolating a scalar error field rather than modifying lookup tables or snapping values. Choose this when data reliability visualization is required. See [Extended Marching Cubes with Error Information](11167-extended-marching-cubes-with-error-information.md).
+### [Signed Distance and Two-Step Methods](10990-signed-distance-and-two-step-methods.md)
+This family extends standard isosurface extraction by leveraging Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) or optimizing implicit function evaluation. It solves computational inefficiency and memory overhead on large-scale data by filtering voxels based on distance to the surface (Narrow-Band) or decoupling expensive implicit function evaluation from geometric extraction (Two-Step). Choose this for high-resolution grids or complex implicit functions. See [Signed Distance and Two-Step Methods](10990-signed-distance-and-two-step-methods.md).
 
-### [Sub-case decomposition for n-dimensional marching cubes](11668-sub-case-decomposition-for-n-dimensional-marching-cubes.md)
-Sub-case decomposition reduces combinatorial explosion in n-dimensional MC by decomposing major cases into edge-connected sub-cases (fragments of boundary) and tetrahedronizing them. It uses a two-level table structure (Primary and Secondary) and pre-computes orientation matrices. Choose this for higher-dimensional isosurface extraction where standard lookup tables are intractable. See [Sub-case decomposition for n-dimensional marching cubes](11668-sub-case-decomposition-for-n-dimensional-marching-cubes.md).
+### [Local Hull-Based Surface Construction](11865-local-hull-based-surface-construction.md)
+Local Hull-Based Surface Construction addresses concavity limitations of standard convex hull algorithms by decomposing an initial surface into local convex regions, constructing a hull for each, and aggregating the results. It differs from siblings by replacing global or local linear interpolation with a two-stage process: classifying local convexities from an initial MC surface, and applying a 3D convex hull algorithm to these subsets to preserve concave features. Choose this when processing volumetric data derived from silhouettes and preserving concave geometry is critical. See [Local Hull-Based Surface Construction](11865-local-hull-based-surface-construction.md).
 
 ### [Edge Groups](14157-edge-groups.md)
-Edge Groups is a classification framework that groups triangle configurations by the connectivity of the cell edges intersected by the isosurface, enabling systematic analysis and bounding of triangle quality. It serves as an analytical tool for understanding and improving MC variants. See [Edge Groups](14157-edge-groups.md).
-
-### [Improved Marching Cubes with New Table and Displacement](14182-improved-marching-cubes-with-new-table-and-displacement.md)
-This variant improves worst-case triangle quality by using a modified MC table that avoids specific edge groups responsible for poor triangles, combined with a non-linear displacement of cut points on edges. Choose this when triangle aspect ratio and quality are critical. See [Improved Marching Cubes with New Table and Displacement](14182-improved-marching-cubes-with-new-table-and-displacement.md).
+Edge Groups is a classification framework that groups triangle configurations by the connectivity of the cell edges intersected by the isosurface, enabling systematic analysis and bounding of triangle quality. It partitions edge configurations into equivalence classes based on cubic cell symmetries. Unlike prior approaches that rely on intuition, it provides a formal combinatorial framework to identify and replace problematic triangulation cases. Choose this as an analytical tool to improve mesh quality in MC variants. See [Edge Groups](14157-edge-groups.md).
 
 ### [Interpolant Induced by Marching Cases](14184-interpolant-induced-by-marching-cases.md)
-This method constructs piecewise-polynomial splines corresponding exactly to MC contours, allowing geometric properties (area, volume) to be computed as continuous functions of the isovalue. Choose this when analytical integration of surface properties is required. See [Interpolant Induced by Marching Cases](14184-interpolant-induced-by-marching-cases.md).
+This method constructs piecewise-polynomial (or non-polynomial) splines that correspond exactly to the contours generated by standard MC cases, enabling the computation of geometric properties (area, volume) as continuous functions of the isovalue. It treats the sequence of Marching Cases as a finite state machine, ensuring the resulting surface is continuous with respect to the isovalue sweep. Choose this when analytical integration of surface properties across a range of isovalues is required. See [Interpolant Induced by Marching Cases](14184-interpolant-induced-by-marching-cases.md).
 
-#### Improved Marching Cubes with Voxel Changes
-**Definition**: An improved MC algorithm that increases the side length of voxels to reduce the number of voxels traversed during 3D reconstruction, thereby improving operation efficiency [S1].
-**Performance**: On the first dataset, traditional MC took 0.809010s, improved MC took 0.314780s (61.0907% efficiency improvement). On the second dataset, traditional MC took 27.291630s, improved MC took 1.763029s (93.5400% efficiency improvement) [S1].
-**Comparison**: Compared to traditional MC, the voxel-change MC has much greater operation efficiency because longer voxel side lengths reduce the number of traversed voxels. The visual difference between the two is smaller when the data amount is larger [S1].
+### [Importance-driven Isosurface Decimation](15016-importance-driven-isosurface-decimation.md)
+Importance-driven isosurface decimation is a parallel mesh simplification strategy that generates decimated meshes progressively without first generating the full-resolution original surface. It uses vertex clustering and crack patching on a hierarchical pyramid structure to enable efficient GPU parallelization. Choose this when processing large scientific datasets that exceed memory limits or hinder interactive visualization. See [Importance-driven Isosurface Decimation](15016-importance-driven-isosurface-decimation.md).
+
+### [Lookup Table and Case Reduction](15794-lookup-table-and-case-reduction.md)
+This family reduces the memory footprint and computational complexity of isosurface extraction by exploiting geometric symmetries to minimize lookup table sizes or by decomposing ambiguous configurations into simpler primitives. It sets itself apart by focusing on combinatorial reduction and topological disambiguation rather than geometric snapping or local hull construction. Choose this when memory bandwidth or table lookup overhead is a bottleneck. See [Lookup Table and Case Reduction](15794-lookup-table-and-case-reduction.md).
+
+#### Single-Entry Cubical Table Marching Cubes
+**Definition**: A disambiguation technique for Marching Cubes used to ensure topological consistency across multiresolution boundaries. It ensures that neighboring cells produce the same contours at a common face despite arbitrary choices at ambiguous faces [S2, unofficial].
+**Usage**: Implemented in the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) as a function called 'synchronized templates' [S2, unofficial].
+
+#### DYNAISOLOD Geometry Generation
+**Definition**: A method that generates triangles on a cell-by-cell basis for cells containing the isosurface, using a modified (disambiguated) triangulation table similar to Marching Cubes [S3, unofficial].
+**Steps**: For each cell, determine which corners are above or below the isovalue to identify intersecting edges. Use the modified triangulation table to decide how to triangulate the isosurface within the cell. Calculate vertex positions by linearly interpolating along the intersecting cell edges. Store calculated vertices in a hash table keyed by the edge they lie on to avoid redundant calculations. Compute normals for each triangle using a cross-product and store them in the same hash table. Optionally, use the hash table to arrange adjacent triangles into triangle strips for more compact representation [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The standard Marching Cubes algorithm, introduced by Lorensen and Cline in 1987, established the lookup-table-based approach for isosurface extraction [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling]. Subsequent research focused on resolving its known issues: topological ambiguities (Cases 46, 47, 98, 99, 210, 213, 214, 215) [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling], poor triangle quality, and computational cost. Variants like SnapMC and Preprocessed MC address mesh quality and robustness, while Bitwise MC and Voxel Changes target performance. Marching Cubes Revisited addresses topological correctness through interval analysis. These modifications reflect the evolution of MC from a basic visualization tool to a robust, high-performance, and metadata-aware geometric processing primitive.
